LIBRARY "r3shieldcore-lib.dll"
EXPORTS
	InjectInit
	GlobalHookSessionStart
	GlobalHookSessionHandleNewProcesses
	GlobalHookSessionDrainEvents
	GlobalHookSessionDroppedEventCount
	GlobalHookSessionSetObserver
	GlobalHookSessionReadPromptStats
	GlobalHookSessionReadHighRiskStats
	GlobalHookSessionEnd
	GlobalHookSessionSetMode
	GlobalHookSessionCheckHighRiskProcess
	GlobalHookSessionSelfExit

; =====================================================================
; !! KEEP THIS FILE PURE ASCII. NO NON-ASCII COMMENTS. !!
; =====================================================================
; New exports go at the END: ordinal numbers are assigned in file order,
; so inserting in the middle shifts every later ordinal.
;
; WHY NO NON-ASCII (tested 2026-09-25, MSVC 14.42, link.exe):
;   A comment line containing certain non-ASCII characters makes link.exe
;   STOP parsing the remaining EXPORTS entries. It emits no error and no
;   warning -- the export is just silently missing from the table.
;   Symptom was: GlobalHookSessionSetMode (the last entry) absent from the
;   built DLL, so the engine's GetProcAddress returned NULL and the GUI
;   mode button did nothing while the console said
;   "DLL does not support runtime mode switching".
;
;   Reproduced with a minimal .def (9 plain exports + 1 comment line +
;   1 export): ASCII comment -> 10 exports; comment with a non-ASCII
;   character -> 9 exports, no diagnostics. The exact triggering character
;   is unstable (some non-ASCII comments are harmless, some are not), so
;   the only safe rule is: keep the whole file ASCII.
;
;   Also verified: the DLL was rebuilt at the right time and the symbol was
;   present in main.obj (dumpbin /symbols) -- so the loss happens purely
;   inside the .def parser. If an export ever "disappears", check this file
;   FIRST with: dumpbin /exports <dll>
; =====================================================================
